The next season, a new spirit arrived at her boundary stone. Mira of the desert wells carried two vessels. "I've heard of your spring," Mira said. "I offer these waters, trusting you'll return them multiplied by your mountain magic."

Andra looked at the two vessels. Once, she would have seen only their potential. Now she saw Mira's vulnerability—the same vulnerability she'd felt sending three vessels to Kelm.

The mountain magic worked. Two vessels became six. But as Andra prepared to return them, she remembered: she had sent three and received back less than three. The mathematics of trust had proven unreliable.

She could return double as promised. She could return what Kelm had returned to her—roughly a third. Or she could find her own path.

Andra sent two vessels back upstream. More than Mira had sent, less than was promised. Enough to keep them both alive, enough that Mira might return next season.

"Is this fair?" Mira asked.

"I don't know," Andra answered honestly. "But it's what I learned: perfect reciprocity is rare. Survival requires accepting less than promised while still giving more than nothing."

